{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":2882423,"items":[{"DOI":"10.1093\/oso\/9780197788332.003.0015","title":["Collective Memory as Collaborative Narration"],"author":[{"given":"Brian","family":"Schiff","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/oso\/9780197788332.003.0015","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,23]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>This chapter argues that a narrative perspective provides the conceptual tools for describing, in detail, the seams or fibers connecting persons and the social world in order to produce commonly agreed-upon understandings, shared meanings, or what the literature calls collective or social memory. Collaborative narratives, in particular, are a privileged space of innovation and negotiation that should serve as a model for thinking about shared meaning. The chapter examines a short segment from the French-language Figaro Live\u2019s Point de Vue, a channel on DirecTV created by the right-leaning newspaper Le Figaro, focusing on a discussion of the great replacement conspiracy theory. It argues that through a narrative lens, collective memory can be thought of as a joint enterprise in sense-making that is fundamentally polysemous and always in motion.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Cognition, Culture, and Political Momentum"]},{"DOI":"10.1093\/oxfordhb\/9780190917982.013.75","title":["Collaborative Remembering and Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Suparna","family":"Rajaram","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Psychology, Stony Brook University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/oxfordhb\/9780190917982.013.75","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,7,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,7,18]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n               <jats:p>A trip down the memory lane is often a social experience. To understand how recalling the past with others shapes one\u2019s memory, it is important to appreciate the reciprocal influences between the collective and the individual. This chapter focuses on cognitive research that examines individual and collective memories that develop in a social context. The studies reviewed in the chapter are typically experimental in nature, although some studies involved other research techniques such as surveys. The theoretical explanations reviewed identify cognitive mechanisms that operate when people engage in collaborative remembering. A confluence of these cognitive mechanisms not only influences the group memory product but also produces post-collaborative changes in what people remember and how they organize these memories. The chapter also discusses research on collaborative remembering across the life span, collaborative memory for emotional information, and the social transmission of false memories. Although collaborative memory studies have used mainly small groups, the chapter also includes recent research that uses larger groups and social networks to understand social transmission of memory. Finally, the chapter focuses on the nature of collective memory. Although collective memory has been a topic of long-held interest in sociology, anthropology, and history, recent research has brought to light psychological perspectives. In the context of studying the cognitive bases of social remembering, the chapter discusses experimental studies that are particularly relevant for understanding the relationship between collaborative remembering and collective memory.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["The Oxford Handbook of Human Memory, Two Volume Pack"]},{"DOI":"10.47297\/wspciwsp2516-252730.20261002","title":["Collaborative Decision-Making Between Large Language Models and Intelligent Agents: A Case Study of DeepSeek"],"author":[{"given":"Wang","family":"Ruikai","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.47297\/wspciwsp2516-252730.20261002","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Creativity and Innovation"]},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781420042597-8","title":["Agents in Adaptive Collaborative Management: the Logic of Collective Cognition"],"author":[{"given":"Niels G.","family":"R\u00f6ling","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Janice","family":"Jiggins","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781420042597-8","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2001,6,21]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2001,6,21]]},"container-title":["Biological Diversity"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781003659792-7","title":["Language, Collective Memory, and Contested Nation-Building"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6072-3874","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ashagrie G.","family":"Abdi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781003659792-7","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,27]]},"container-title":["Memory Politics and Building Multinational States in Africa"]},{"DOI":"10.1080\/09658211.2023.2267190","title":["Collective interactions, collaborative inhibition, and shared spatial knowledge"],"author":[{"given":"Teriitutea","family":"Quesnot","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Univ Brest, CNRS, LETG \u2013 UMR 6554, Plouzan\u00e9, France"},{"name":"Institut Universitaire de France, Paris, France"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Bernard","family":"Guelton","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Univ Paris 1, Institut ACTE \u2013 UR 7539, Paris, France"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/09658211.2023.2267190","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,11,26]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2023,10,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,10,20]]},"container-title":["Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5175593","title":["Construction of Collaborative Intelligent Manufacturing Agents\u00a0For Special Transformer Coil\u00a0Workshop\u00a0Based on Large Language Models\u00a0And Digital Twins"],"author":[{"given":"Xuedong","family":"Zhang","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Wenlei","family":"Sun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ke","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Renben","family":"Jiang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5175593","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.1201\/9781420042597.ch8","title":["Agents in Adaptive Collaborative Management"],"author":[{"given":"Niels","family":"R\u00f6ling","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Janice","family":"Jiggins","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1201\/9781420042597.ch8","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2001,6,21]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2009,11,23]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2001,6,21]]},"container-title":["Biological Diversity"]},{"DOI":"10.21832\/9781788925402-014","title":["9. Efficacy Towards Collaborative Practice and Collective Action"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21832\/9781788925402-014","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,12,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,12,31]]},"container-title":["Exploring Language Teacher Efficacy in Japan"]},{"DOI":"10.31234\/osf.io\/km6jx_v1","title":["Collective Memory Modeling: Utilizing Individualized Representations to Drive Processing Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-6152-4914","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Brendan","family":"Johns","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31234\/osf.io\/km6jx_v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,4]]},"abstract":"<p>This article advances a new framework for computational memory modeling that integrates individual variability in semantic representation with computational models of memory processes. Although existing approaches to computational memory modeling have provided detailed accounts of encoding, retrieval, and decision dynamics, they typically assume fixed representations, overlooking individual differences in linguistic experience. Focusing on false memory in the Deese\u2013Roediger\u2013McDermott (DRM; Roediger &amp;amp; McDermott, 1995) paradigm, individualized distributional semantic models were constructed from social media sources and embedded within the Minerva 2 model of recognition memory while holding process parameters constant. This design isolates the contribution of representational variability to memory performance. Across simulations of 2,000 individual models, substantial variability emerged in item-level predictions. When using an optimized subset of individuals models, aggregated predictions produced strong correspondence with item-level data and exhibited a robust wisdom of the crowd effect (Steyvers &amp;amp; Miller, 2015), where the aggregated item-level data exceeded the performance of the best performing individual model. Additional analyses demonstrated that aggregation stabilizes behavioral predictions while preserving meaningful variability arising from differences in semantic structure. These findings provide an existence proof that representational diversity alone can account for systematic variation in memory behavior and improve predictive accuracy of a proposed model. More broadly, the results highlight the importance of incorporating individualized semantic knowledge into computational models, offering a unified and ecologically valid framework that bridges representation and process in the study of human memory.<\/p>"},{"DOI":"10.1080\/09658210701811862","title":["Collaborative recall and collective memory: What happens when we remember together?"],"author":[{"given":"Celia B.","family":"Harris","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Helen M.","family":"Paterson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Richard I.","family":"Kemp","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/09658210701811862","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2008,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2008,4]]},"container-title":["Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.2307\/jj.22730652.16","title":["Efficacy Towards Collaborative Practice and Collective Action"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2307\/jj.22730652.16","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,5,14]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,5,14]]},"container-title":["Exploring Language Teacher Efficacy in Japan"]},{"DOI":"10.3167\/isr.2020.350102","title":["Jerusalem\u2019s Alternative Collective Memory Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Menachem","family":"Klein","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Bar-Ilan University, Israel kleinm11@gmail.com"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3167\/isr.2020.350102","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,3,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Jerusalem played an important role in the establishment of collective memory studies by Maurice Halbwachs in the early twentieth century. Recent studies in this field draw attention to the contribution of a variety of agents to building, maintaining, and challenging collective memory realms. Following suit, this article deals with the methods that agents of an alternative collective memory for Jerusalem use to challenge the Israeli hegemonic narrative. Before reviewing their activities in East and West Jerusalem and their resources and impact, I summarize the hegemonic narrative as presented in four memory realms. Special attention is given to both sides\u2019 use of the Internet as a means of overcoming the physical limitations of memory realms.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Israel Studies Review"]},{"DOI":"10.26855\/ea.2026.06.011","title":["Research on Memory Management and Dynamic Reasoning Methods for Intelligent Agents Based on Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Yiling","family":"Yuan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.26855\/ea.2026.06.011","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]]},"container-title":["Engineering Advances"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11391226","title":["A Hybrid, Multi-Layered Memory Architecture for Collaborative Reasoning in Multi-Agent Systems"],"author":[{"given":"Maksim","family":"Ilin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Transport and Telecommunication Institute,Riga,Latvia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Dmitry","family":"Pavlyuk","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Transport and Telecommunication Institute,Riga,Latvia"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/fllm67465.2025.11391226","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,25]]},"container-title":["2025 3rd International Conference on Foundation and Large Language Models (FLLM)"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781003357360-9","title":["Persistence of false memories and emergence of collective false memory: collaborative recall of DRM word lists"],"author":[{"given":"Raeya","family":"Maswood","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Christian C.","family":"Luhmann","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Suparna","family":"Rajaram","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781003357360-9","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,12,30]]},"container-title":["Memory Online"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>In social learning models, truth-seeking agents learn both individually from direct evidence and socially by pooling beliefs with others. That learning can be undermined by two types of unreliable agents: zealots, who do not learn and promote the same fixed opinion and free riders, who lack access to evidence yet still influence others. In this paper, we explore how learning rules that incorporate memory can mitigate the effects of unreliable agents. To do so, we construct an agent-based model of social learning in which agents apply a probabilistic bounded confidence (BC) model that evaluates the similarity between themselves and others based on samples of recent beliefs rather than current beliefs only. When compared to a memoryless BC benchmark, BC with memory proves significantly less sensitive to the choice of similarity threshold governing agent interactions, to the extent that a fixed threshold is effective for avoiding all types of zealots. It is also less susceptible to high levels of distrust in evidence. The BC with memory model is then extended to social learning about multiple hypotheses, and we show that the robustness results generalise to the case in which beliefs are multi-dimensional probability distributions.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1177\/26339137261429497","title":["Incorporating memory into bounded confidence models of probabilistic social learning"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-3488-5682","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jonathan","family":"Lawry","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Bristol"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,2]]},"container-title":["Collective Intelligence"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1177\/26339137261429497"},{"DOI":"10.1038\/s41598-022-21452-5","title":["Infants expect agents to minimize the collective cost of collaborative actions"],"author":[{"given":"Olivier","family":"Mascaro","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Gergely","family":"Csibra","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1038\/s41598-022-21452-5","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2022,10,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,10,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title><jats:p>This paper argues that human infants address the challenges of optimizing, recognizing, and interpreting collaborative behaviors by assessing their collective efficiency. This hypothesis was tested by using a looking-time study. Fourteen-month-olds (<jats:italic>N<\/jats:italic>\u2009=\u200932) were familiarized with agents performing a collaborative action in computer animations. During the test phase, the looking times were measured while the agents acted with various efficiency parameters. In the critical condition, the agents\u2019 actions were individually efficient, but their combination was either collectively efficient or inefficient. Infants looked longer at test events that violated expectations of collective efficiency (<jats:italic>p<\/jats:italic>\u2009=\u2009.006,<jats:italic>d<\/jats:italic>\u2009=\u20090.79). Thus, preverbal infants apply expectations of collective efficiency to actions involving multiple agents.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Scientific Reports"]},{"DOI":"10.1080\/09658211.2021.1928222","title":["Persistence of false memories and emergence of collective false memory: collaborative recall of DRM word lists"],"author":[{"given":"Raeya","family":"Maswood","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Psychology, Stony Brook University, Stony Brook, NY, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Christian C.","family":"Luhmann","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Psychology, Stony Brook University, Stony Brook, NY, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Suparna","family":"Rajaram","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Psychology, Stony Brook University, Stony Brook, NY, USA"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1080\/09658211.2021.1928222","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,4,21]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2021,5,26]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,5,26]]},"container-title":["Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2023.findings-emnlp.226","title":["Open-Ended Instructable Embodied Agents with Memory-Augmented Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Gabriel","family":"Sarch","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Yue","family":"Wu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Michael","family":"Tarr","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Katerina","family":"Fragkiadaki","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2023.findings-emnlp.226","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Findings of the Association for Computational Linguistics: EMNLP 2023"]},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2025.realm-1.3","title":["CAMPHOR: Collaborative Agents for Multi-input Planning and High-Order Reasoning On Device"],"author":[{"given":"Yicheng","family":"Fu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Raviteja","family":"Anantha","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jianpeng","family":"Cheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2025.realm-1.3","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 1st Workshop for Research on Agent Language Models (REALM 2025)"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2015.05.003","title":["Why does collaborative retrieval improve memory? Enhanced relational and item-specific processing"],"author":[{"given":"Kathryn T.","family":"Wissman","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Katherine A.","family":"Rawson","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2015.05.003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2015,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2015,10]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2022.104373","title":["Agents\u2019 goals affect construal of event endpoints"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1053-5813","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Ariel","family":"Mathis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Anna","family":"Papafragou","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2022.104373","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,12]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.4018\/978-1-59140-845-1.ch008","title":["Simulation Modelling within Collaborative Spatial Decision Support Systems Using \"Cause-Effect\" Models and Software Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Raja","family":"Sengupta","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"McGill University, Canada"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4018\/978-1-59140-845-1.ch008","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2006]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2006]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Solutions to spatial environmental problems often require the integration of dynamic simulation models within GIS to create spatial decision support systems (SDSS) that can generate responses to theoretical \u201dWhat if?\u201d scenarios. Extending this paradigm to a collaborative spatial decision support system, however, faces significant challenges. This includes the inability of computationally intensive models to provide real-time results, and the inability of novice end users to effectively parameterize the models. Effective solutions to these problems proposed here include the use of \u201dcause-effect\u201d models to link inputs to outputs for a limited number of scenarios, as well as utilizing software agents that assist novice users in determining the correct input parameters for the models. Examples from the St-Esprit watershed SDSS serve to elucidate the proposed solutions.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Collaborative Geographic Information Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-642-21178-2_14","title":["Collaborative Translation Protocols"],"author":[{"given":"Daisuke","family":"Morita","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Toru","family":"Ishida","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-642-21178-2_14","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2011]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2011,7,16]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2011]]},"container-title":["Cognitive Technologies","The Language Grid"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9780203015964-7","title":["Collective memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9780203015964-7","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2007,11,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2007,11,13]]},"container-title":["The Obituary as Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2006.08.006","title":["Some targets for memory models"],"author":[{"given":"Stephan","family":"Lewandowsky","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Evan","family":"Heit","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2006.08.006","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2006,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2006,11]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title><jats:p>Collective memory is shared by a group and is part of that group\u2019s identity. Memory for political leaders is a prototypical case of collective memory. The present study investigated collective memory for Swiss federal councilors in order to test the trajectory of collective memory across four different generations (i.e., Millennials, Generation X, Baby-Boomers, and Silents) in a collaborative government system. In contrast to a presidential system, Switzerland is governed by seven equal councilors who share power and responsibilities. Thus, the individual member of the government is less important, and the number of councilors is larger compared to a presidential system, which may influence collective memory. The results revealed a recency effect as well as a generation-specific reminiscence effect, but no primacy effect as reported for presidential systems. These results indicate that the contribution of semantic memory and autobiographic memory to the trajectory of collective memory vary across government systems. Specifically, for a collaborative government system, autobiographic memory has a stronger contribution to the trajectory of collective memory.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.3758\/s13421-020-01076-8","title":["Collective memory for political leaders in a collaborative government system: Evidence for generation-specific reminiscence effects"],"author":[{"given":"Beat","family":"Meier","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2020,8,5]]},"container-title":["Memory &amp; Cognition"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,8,5]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3758\/s13421-020-01076-8"},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2025.emnlp-main.249","title":["Collab-Overcooked: Benchmarking and Evaluating Large Language Models as Collaborative Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Haochen","family":"Sun","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shuwen","family":"Zhang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lujie","family":"Niu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Lei","family":"Ren","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hao","family":"Xu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Hao","family":"Fu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Fangkun","family":"Zhao","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Caixia","family":"Yuan","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Xiaojie","family":"Wang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2025.emnlp-main.249","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 2025 Conference on Empirical Methods in Natural Language Processing"]},{"DOI":"10.2190\/y2mn-mh00-d6g5-5a6g","title":["Review of Traditional and Collaborative Models for Negotiation"],"author":[{"family":"James Kelleher","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2190\/y2mn-mh00-d6g5-5a6g","issued":{"date-parts":[[2000,12,1]]},"container-title":["Journal of Collective Negotiations (formerly Journal of Collective Negotiations in the Public Sector)"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-0515-8_3","title":["LangChain and Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Pere","family":"Martra","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-0515-8_3","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Large Language Models Projects"]},{"DOI":"10.5040\/9781350477469.ch-9","title":["Shaping collective memory: The role of narrative composition in historical representation and identity"],"author":[{"given":"Orsolya","family":"Vincze","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5040\/9781350477469.ch-9","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Language and Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-1-4614-4951-5_51","title":["Analyzing Agents\u2019 Collective Design Decisions in Collaborative Systems Development Initiatives"],"author":[{"given":"Majed","family":"Al-Shawa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-1-4614-4951-5_51","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2013]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2013,4,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2013]]},"container-title":["Information Systems Development"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2012.06.001","title":["Managing dialogue: How information availability affects collaborative reference production"],"author":[{"given":"Dominique","family":"Knutsen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Ludovic","family":"Le Bigot","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2012.06.001","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2012,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2012,10]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1136\/bmjspcare-2017-hospice.266","title":["P-241\u2005A collective approach to service development"],"author":[{"given":"Vanessa","family":"Gibson","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Sarah Popplestone","family":"Helm","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1136\/bmjspcare-2017-hospice.266","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,11]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2017,11,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017,11]]},"container-title":["Service development, models and collaborative working"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781003374527-9","title":["From collective memory to collective identity"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9390-1585","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Pavel","family":"Brunssen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781003374527-9","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,22]]},"container-title":["Football, Fandom and Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.36227\/techrxiv.175613226.63459477\/v1","title":["Persistent Memory Logic Loop (PMLL) Architecture: Memory Footprint Reduction in Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-3414-7949","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Josef Kurk","family":"Edwards","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute for Advanced AI Systems, Stanford University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.36227\/techrxiv.175613226.63459477\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,8,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large-scale Transformer-based language models (LLMs) such as GPT-3 and GPT-4 require substantial memory resources, with GPT-3's 175 billion parameters demanding approximately 356 GB at 16-bit precision. This memory burden stems from dense weight matrices, linearly-growing key-value (KV) caches, and redundant knowledge re-encoding across inference cycles. We introduce the Persistent Memory Logic Loop (PMLL), a novel architecture that augments standard Transformers with an external, compressed persistent memory pool, queue-theoretic promise semantics, and recursive compression algorithms. PMLL achieves a 59-60% reduction in memory footprint while maintaining accuracy within 1.5% of baseline models. Our approach combines modular placement using collision-free hash functions, importance-weighted pruning, vector quantization, and memory-efficient attention mechanisms. Experimental validation on WikiText-2, PG-19, and OpenWebText datasets demonstrates consistent performance gains. Additionally, we present a novel Fourier-Hypotenuse Path Refinement algorithm for the Traveling Salesman Problem that achieves within 1.5% of optimal solutions using O(N) memory. This work provides both theoretical foundations and production-ready implementations for deploying memory-efficient LLMs at scale.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1109\/cic58953.2023.00021","title":["Enabling Synergistic Knowledge Sharing and Reasoning in Large Language Models with Collaborative Multi-Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Ayushman","family":"Das","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Missouri-Kansas City,Data Science and Analytics Innovation Center,Kansas City,MO,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Shu-Ching","family":"Chen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Missouri-Kansas City,Data Science and Analytics Innovation Center,Kansas City,MO,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mei-Ling","family":"Shyu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Science and Engineering, University of Missouri-Kansas City,Kansas City,MO,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Saad","family":"Sadiq","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Microsoft,Seattle,WA,USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/cic58953.2023.00021","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,11,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,11,1]]},"container-title":["2023 IEEE 9th International Conference on Collaboration and Internet Computing (CIC)"]},{"DOI":"10.2307\/jj.27024376.11","title":["Media and Collective Memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2307\/jj.27024376.11","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,22]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,4,22]]},"container-title":["Rainforest Radio"]},{"DOI":"10.3726\/b19946","title":["Language Attitudes, Collective Memory and (Trans)National Identity Construction Among the Armenian Diaspora in Bulgaria"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3726\/b19946","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>This book examines the processes of symbolic cultivation of identity promoted by Armenian cultural elites in the Bulgarian city of Plovdiv, focusing on the transmission of positive language ideologies and emotional elements related to collective memory. Drawing on extensive ethnographic fieldwork, interviews, and a range of primary materials, this work sheds light on the role of the Armenian alphabet in legitimizing collective visions of \u2018distinctiveness\u2019 and of the Armenian Genocide remembrance in shaping non-exclusive, transnational patterns of belonging. While contributing to the study of the complex dynamics and challenges of \u2018Armenian survival\u2019 across space and time, it situates the issue in the unique context of Bulgaria, analyzing, moreover, the impact of proximity to Turkey.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.7454\/jipk.v27i1.1127","title":["Analysis of Collaborative Governance of The Nation's Collective Memory Archive Registration Program"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7454\/jipk.v27i1.1127","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,1]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,5,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Archive Registration as Collective Memory of the Nation is one of the efforts to preserve the documentary heritage of the Indonesian nation. In its implementation, the National Archives of the Republic of Indonesia involves non-governmental institutions, namely state universities and history and culture activists in the nomination, and also involves community organizations, political organizations, and companies in mobilizing participation. This collaborative governance has an impact on the implementation of the Archive Registration as Collective Memory of the Nation program. The research question is how is the implementation of Collaborative Governance in the program and what factors hinder its implementation. The purpose of the study is to implement the application of Collaborative Governance in the Archive Registration as Collective Memory of the Nation Program and to identify the factors that hinder its implementation. The implementation of the program is explained using the theory of Ansell and Gash, namely the initial aspects in the form of successful face-to-face dialogue, building trust, shared understanding, commitment to the process, and intermediate results. This study uses a descriptive research method with a qualitative approach. Researchers collect data using secondary data and review relevant literature on collaborative governance, archiving, and the nation's collective memory. The results of this study indicate that the implementation of Collaborative Governance in the Archives Registration Program as the Nation's Collective Memory has been running well, but needs to be optimized by improving the inhibiting factors in its implementation.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Jurnal Ilmu Informasi, Perpustakaan, dan Kearsipan"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5183663","title":["Leveraging Large Language Models for Collective Decision-Making"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-1728-0729","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Marios","family":"Papachristou","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6615-8615","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Longqi","family":"Yang","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0144-9822","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Chin-Chia","family":"Hsu","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5183663","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>In various work contexts, such as meeting scheduling, collaborating, and project planning, collective decisionmaking is essential but often challenging due to diverse individual preferences, varying work focuses, and power dynamics among members. To address this, we propose a system leveraging Large Language Models (LLMs) to facilitate group decision-making by managing conversations and balancing preferences among individuals. Our system aims to extract individual preferences from each member's conversation with the system and suggest options that satisfy the preferences of the members. We specifically apply this system to corporate meeting scheduling. We create synthetic employee profiles and simulate conversations at scale, leveraging LLMs to evaluate the system performance as a novel approach to conducting a user study. Our results indicate efficient coordination with reduced interactions between the members and the LLM-based system. The system refines and improves its proposed options over time, ensuring that many of the members' individual preferences are satisfied in an equitable way. Finally, we conduct a survey study involving human participants to assess our system's ability to aggregate preferences and reasoning about them. Our findings show that the system exhibits strong performance in both dimensions.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.3998\/15499139.0005.013","title":["Transforming the Expression of Collective Memory in Sign Language"],"author":[{"given":"Ted","family":"Supalla","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3998\/15499139.0005.013","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,19]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,6,19]]},"container-title":["Deaf Studies Digital Journal"]},{"DOI":"10.31224\/6851","title":["Integrating Graphs, Large Language Models, and Agents: Reasoning and Retrieval"],"author":[{"given":"Hamed","family":"Jelodar","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.31224\/6851","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,4,19]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Generative AI particularly Large Language Models, increasingly integrates graph-based representations to enhance reasoning, retrieval, and structured decision-making. Despite rapid advances, there remains limited clarity regarding when, why, where, and what types of graph\u2013LLM integrations are most appropriate across applications. This survey provides a concise, structured overview of the design choices underlying the integration of graphs with LLMs. We categorize existing methods based on their purpose (e.g., reasoning, retrieval, generation, recommendation), graph modality (knowledge graphs, scene graphs, interaction graphs, causal graphs, dependency graphs), and integration strategies (prompting, augmentation, training, or agent-based use). By mapping representative works across domains such as cybersecurity, healthcare, materials science, finance, robotics, and multimodal environments, we highlight the strengths, limitations, and best-fit scenarios for each technique. This survey aims to offer researchers a practical guide for selecting the most suitable graph\u2013LLM approach depending on task requirements, data characteristics, and reasoning complexity.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.7494\/human.2024.23.2.6654","title":["Collective Memory or Collective Oblivion? The Case of Holocaust Memory In Three Polish Localities"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8057-7226","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Rados\u0142aw","family":"Tyra\u0142a","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7494\/human.2024.23.2.6654","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,1,31]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>The aim of this article is to show the scope and content of the memory of Jewish deportations and the Holo caust today through a study conducted in three Polish towns of varying sizes with a rich Jewish past (Krak\u00f3w, Nowy S\u0105cz, Muszyna) which I surveyed in 2023. Additionally, I wanted to identify the channels in which the memory of the Holocaust and deportations circulates in the surveyed localities. The research was conducted with representatives of three generations, so it is also important to show how age differentiates the memory of Jewish deportation and the Holocaust. Both age and the size of the locality turn out to be important in dif ferentiating the extent of this memory. Moreover, an important conclusion of the study is that the memory of those events is limited and subject to repression, at risk of falling into oblivion. Relatively speaking, most of the memory of those events is preserved in larger cities among representatives of the middle generation, which is related to the existence of material memory infrastructures there facilitating the formation of local communities of memory of Jews and the Holocaust. Based on the three focus group interviews I conducted, it can be concluded that the dominant channel of memory transfer about local Jewish history and the Holocaust is communicative memory. Conversations about Jews and the Holocaust, if they do occur, generally happen with family members or acquaintances, but relatively rarely at school. Generally, conversations are initiated by people already interested in the topic and here the role of communities of memory, such as the S\u0105decki Shtetl, is very important.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Studia Humanistyczne AGH"]},{"DOI":"10.1163\/9789087909604_003","title":["Co-optation, Ethical Dilemmas and Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Brian D.","family":"Schultz","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Paris","family":"Banks","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1163\/9789087909604_003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2009,1,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2009,1,1]]},"container-title":["The Collaborative Turn"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.6456718","title":["Weighted Developmental Memory Architecture (WDMA): A Theoretical Framework for Cost-Aware, Self-Evolving Memory Systems in Large Language Model Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2582-4594","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"Saurav","family":"Das","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.6456718","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly operate across extended time horizons, yet their memory systems remain poorly suited to sustained, goal-directed tasks. Most existing approaches optimize for recall quality in isolation, neglecting the intertwined costs of latency, token consumption, and temporal coherence. We introduce the Weighted Developmental Memory Architecture (WDMA), a theoretical framework that models agent memory as a five-layer, salience-gated system governed by thermodynamic efficiency principles. WDMA introduces three core contributions: (1) a jolt-based encoding mechanism in which memories are formed proportional to prediction-error magnitude, with thresholds that co-evolve with domain competence; (2) a D0-D5 expansion taxonomy that classifies synthetic memory training data by semantic transformation depth, from raw replay to calibration signals; and (3) a promotion utility function U (m) that scores memory entries for layer advancement based on recency, novelty, correctness, and access cost. We further characterize three failure modes-self-confirmation loops, stale-memory contamination, and false causal attribution-and propose architectural mitigations for each. While empirical validation remains future work, WDMA provides a principled vocabulary and design space for researchers building the next generation of persistent, adaptive AI agents.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781108955638.011","title":["Computational Models of Working Memory for Language"],"author":[{"given":"Graham J.","family":"Hitch","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Mark J.","family":"Hurlstone","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Tom","family":"Hartley","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781108955638.011","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,7,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,7,31]]},"container-title":["The Cambridge Handbook of Working Memory and Language"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                    This article explores how Russian disinformation networks exploit large language models (LLMs) to advance what Timothy Snyder, in his book\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">The Road to Unfreedom<\/jats:italic>\n                    , describes as \u2018the politics of eternity\u2019 \u2013 a worldview that replaces progress with cyclical narratives of national victimhood and mythologised pasts, thereby legitimising authoritarian permanence. Drawing parallels with Orwell's concept of manipulated social memory in\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">1984<\/jats:italic>\n                    , the article examines how the contamination of AI training data represents a new phase in information warfare. Unlike traditional propaganda, tainted datasets embed lasting distortions in digital memory, subtly reshaping how societies understand history and truth. By examining examples of LLM manipulation, the article shows how such systems are used to amplify the Kremlin's message and narrative, and to erode fact-based discourse. This technological evolution reveals how modern information infrastructures are being weaponised to fragment shared reality and thereby to weaken the potential for political resistance.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1177\/29768640251377941","title":["Revisionist future: Russia's assault on large language models, the distortion of collective memory, and the politics of eternity"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-4154-2300","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Huw","family":"Dylan","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"King's College London"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-0148-2956","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Elena","family":"Grossfeld","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"King's College London"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,29]]},"container-title":["Dialogues on Digital Society"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,29]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1177\/29768640251377941"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781316809600.003","title":["Collective Memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781316809600.003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"container-title":["Collective Remembering"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/slt.2010.5700855","title":["A collective data generation method for speech language models"],"author":[{"given":"Sean","family":"Liu","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Stephanie","family":"Seneff","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"James","family":"Glass","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/slt.2010.5700855","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2010,12]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2010,12]]},"container-title":["2010 IEEE Spoken Language Technology Workshop"]},{"DOI":"10.1145\/3643562.3672609","title":["Towards Collaborative Brain-storming among Humans and AI Agents: An Implementation of the IBIS-based Brainstorming Support System with Multiple AI Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-0720-3483","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Moeka","family":"Nomura","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Ito Laboratory, Computer Science Course, Undergraduate School of Informatics and Mathematical Science, Kyoto University, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5093-3886","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Takayuki","family":"Ito","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Ito Laboratory, Computer Science Course, Undergraduate School of Informatics and Mathematical Science, Kyoto University, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-6329-2310","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Shiyao","family":"Ding","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Ito Laboratory, Computer Science Course, Undergraduate School of Informatics and Mathematical Science, Kyoto University, Japan"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1145\/3643562.3672609","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,27]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,7,18]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,6,27]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the ACM Collective Intelligence Conference"]},{"DOI":"10.21832\/9781783095735-005","title":["2. Working Memory Theories and Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21832\/9781783095735-005","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2016,12,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2016,12,31]]},"container-title":["Working Memory and Second Language Learning"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2020.104195","title":["Evaluating theories of bilingual language control using computational models"],"author":[{"given":"Mark","family":"Lowry","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Chad","family":"Dub\u00e9","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Elizabeth","family":"Schotter","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2020.104195","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,4]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781315794495","title":["Connectionist Models of Memory and Language (PLE: Memory)"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781315794495","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2014,5,9]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2014,5,9]]}},{"DOI":"10.1007\/979-8-8688-0110-5_4","title":["Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Michael","family":"McTear","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Marina","family":"Ashurkina","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/979-8-8688-0110-5_4","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,2,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Transforming Conversational AI"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0014820500004018","title":["Collaborative Construction of Domain Ontologies Supported by Evidence-Grounded Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Silvia","family":"Borowicc","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Arts, Sciences and Humanities, Universidade de S\u00e3o Paulo, Arlindo Bettio 1000, S\u00e3o Paulo, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Wesley","family":"Barbosa","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Polytechnic School, Universidade de S\u00e3o Paulo, S\u00e3o Paulo, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Solange","family":"Alves-Souza","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"School of Arts, Sciences and Humanities, Universidade de S\u00e3o Paulo, Arlindo Bettio 1000, S\u00e3o Paulo, Brazil"},{"name":"Polytechnic School, Universidade de S\u00e3o Paulo, S\u00e3o Paulo, Brazil"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0014820500004018","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 28th International Conference on Enterprise Information Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1039\/d4sc03921a\/v2\/review2","title":["Review for \"A Review of Large Language Models and Autonomous Agents in Chemistry\""],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1039\/d4sc03921a\/v2\/review2","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,12,3]]}},{"DOI":"10.1039\/d4sc03921a\/v1\/review1","title":["Review for \"A Review of Large Language Models and Autonomous Agents in Chemistry\""],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1039\/d4sc03921a\/v1\/review1","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,3]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,3]]}},{"DOI":"10.1039\/d4sc03921a\/v2\/review1","title":["Review for \"A Review of Large Language Models and Autonomous Agents in Chemistry\""],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1039\/d4sc03921a\/v2\/review1","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,13]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,11,13]]}},{"DOI":"10.1039\/d4sc03921a\/v1\/review2","title":["Review for \"A Review of Large Language Models and Autonomous Agents in Chemistry\""],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1039\/d4sc03921a\/v1\/review2","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,9,11]]}},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,3]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>ABSTRACT<\/jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) have dramatically transformed the AI landscape. They can produce remarkable fluent text and exhibit a range of natural language understanding and generation capabilities. This article explores how LLMs might be used for sociolinguistic research and, conversely, how sociolinguistics can contribute to the development of LLMs. It argues that both areas of research will benefit from a thoughtful, engaging collaboration. Sociolinguists are not merely end users of LLMs; they have a crucial role to play in the development of LLMs.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1111\/lnc3.70010","title":["Collaborative Growth: When Large Language Models Meet Sociolinguistics"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-6062-3117","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Dong","family":"Nguyen","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Information and Computing Sciences Faculty of Science Utrecht University  Utrecht The Netherlands"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,2,3]]},"container-title":["Language and Linguistics Compass"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,2,3]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1111\/lnc3.70010"},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-642-23605-1_14","title":["Modeling and Analyzing Agents\u2019 Collective Options in Collaborative Decision Making"],"author":[{"given":"Majed","family":"Al-Shawa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-642-23605-1_14","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2011]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2011]]},"container-title":["Lecture Notes in Computer Science","Brain Informatics"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2025.104677","title":["Language models that match reader experience are better predictors of reading times"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-7044-8957","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Iza","family":"\u0160krjanec","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vera","family":"Demberg","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2025.104677","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,2]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,2]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.1006\/jmla.1999.2703","title":["Linguistic vs. Conceptual Sources of Implicit Agents in Sentence Comprehension"],"author":[{"given":"Gail","family":"Mauner","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Jean-Pierre","family":"Koenig","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1006\/jmla.1999.2703","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2000,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2000,7]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9780203888049-11","title":["COLLECTIVE MEMORY"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9780203888049-11","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2008,9,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2008,9,4]]},"container-title":["Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2007.11.004","title":["Models of accuracy in repeated-measures designs"],"author":[{"given":"Peter","family":"Dixon","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2007.11.004","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2008,11]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2008,11]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025,12]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\n                    As the war in Ukraine unfolds, the Hungarian government seems less enthusiastic about the idea of state sovereignty than one might have anticipated given Hungary\u2019s historic past. Moreover, there is a surge of pro-Russian sentiment among government supporters. This is counterintuitive in light of prevailing mnemonic practices which put heavy emphasis on Hungary\u2019s struggle for freedom and independence. The new developments suggest that the role of collective memory in collective identity formation needs to be reconsidered, especially concerning the apparent inhibition of otherwise persistent and widely shared memories. The article discusses the problem in the context of\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">socially shared retrieval-induced forgetting<\/jats:italic>\n                    and\n                    <jats:italic toggle=\"yes\">social identity complexity<\/jats:italic>\n                    theory. It puts forward two complementary arguments: (1) this Janus-faced public behaviour might be accounted for by selective commemorative practices of the government and (2) how memories of historical events sustain a shared sense of belonging and continuity is not only contingent on group membership but also on the ever-changing group interest.\n                  <\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1177\/17506980241283893","title":["Whatever happened to Hungarian freedom fighters? Collective memory, collective forgetting, and dissociative collective identity"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1429-0689","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Daniel","family":"Gyollai","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Copenhagen, Denmark"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,10,6]]},"container-title":["Memory Studies"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,10,6]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1177\/17506980241283893"},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023,7]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title><jats:p>Memory researchers and theorists have long advanced the idea that the manner in which information is retrieved is critical. The way retrieval unfolds provides critical insights into how memories are organized and accessed\u2014an important aspect of memory missed by focusing only on quantity. Cognitive studies of memory in social contexts, deploying the collaborative memory paradigm, have also noted the importance of such retrieval organization. Such memory studies often focus on how relative to \u201cgroups\u201d that never collaborated, former members of collaborating groups recall more of the same material (<jats:italic>collective memory<\/jats:italic>) and they do so in a more synchronized fashion (<jats:italic>collective retrieval organization<\/jats:italic>). In this review, we leverage the diverse methodological and quantitative toolkits that have traditionally targeted individual retrieval to highlight the ways in which this social memory research has examined collective memory and collective retrieval organization. To that end, we consider how the collaborative memory paradigm has integrated <jats:italic>methods<\/jats:italic>, such as free recall, that afford rich assessments of retrieval organization. Likewise, we consider the application of <jats:italic>metrics<\/jats:italic> that characterize organization patterns in different contexts. With this background in mind, we discuss the important theoretical and broader implications of research on collective memory and collective retrieval organization.<\/jats:p><jats:p>This article is categorized under:<jats:list list-type=\"simple\">\n<jats:list-item><jats:p>Psychology &gt; Memory<\/jats:p><\/jats:list-item>\n<\/jats:list><\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1002\/wcs.1641","title":["Collective memory: Collaborative recall synchronizes what and how people remember"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-1350-4227","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Garrett D.","family":"Greeley","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Psychology Stony Brook University  Stony Brook New York USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-8947-1022","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Suparna","family":"Rajaram","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Psychology Stony Brook University  Stony Brook New York USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2023,1,12]]},"container-title":["WIREs Cognitive Science"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023,1,12]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1002\/wcs.1641"},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0012607400003636","title":["Explainable Large Language Models &amp; iContracts"],"author":[{"given":"Georgios","family":"Stathis","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Institute of Tax Law and Economics, Leiden University, Kamerlingh Onnes Building, Steenschuur 25, Leiden, The Netherlands, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0012607400003636","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 16th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.5318129","title":["RLLM: A Collaborative Review Protocol for Enhancing Decision-Making in Large Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Lefliti","family":"Mohamed","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.5318129","issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]}},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0011781800003393","title":["Blending Dependency Parsers With Language Models"],"author":[{"given":"Nicos","family":"Isaak","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Computational Cognition Lab, Cyprus, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0011781800003393","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2023]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 15th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1093\/oso\/9780198737865.003.0016","title":["Collective Memory: How Groups Remember Their Past"],"author":[{"given":"Magdalena","family":"Abel","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Sharda","family":"Umanath","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"James V.","family":"Wertsch","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Henry L.","family":"Roediger","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1093\/oso\/9780198737865.003.0016","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,12,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017,12,7]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract<\/jats:title>\n               <jats:p>Studies of collective memory address how people create and maintain a shared representation of their group\u2019s past and group identity. In particular, we conside how knowledge representations and schematic narrative templates (recurring stories of the past) contribute to collective remembering. Diverging memories between groups can cause conflict, so examining how different group\u2019s varying memories of \u201cthe same event\u201d can cause misunderstandings is critical. We consider whether (and how) groups can mediate their differences to attempt to reach consensus about the past, using narratives of World War II as a case study. The study of collective memory comprises many different senses of the term remembering, and this chapter emphasizes the benefits of interdisciplinary collaboration to examine the issues from multiple perspectives.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["Collaborative Remembering"]},{"DOI":"10.22541\/au.177430006.66800854\/v1","title":["Robust Collaborative Framework for Consistent Undergraduate Mathematical Reasoning with Large Language Models"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0004-8390-8942","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Zixuan","family":"Shi","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Xihua University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Mengyao","family":"Qian","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Xihua University"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.177430006.66800854\/v1","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,3,23]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Large Language Models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive capabilities in diverse reasoning tasks, yet they struggle with the consistency and robustness required for complex mathematical reasoning, especially at the undergraduate level. Benchmarks such as UGMathBench reveal LLMs' significant instability when faced with minor variable perturbations in semantically similar problems, leading to low Effective Accuracy (EAcc) and a substantial Reasoning Gap. To overcome this limitation, we introduce the Robust Collaborative Mathematical Reasoning (RCMR-Math) framework. RCMR-Math integrates multimodal collaborative reasoning, multi-view verification utilizing external tools, and an iterative self-correction mechanism. Comprising five interconnected modules, the framework aims for enhanced consistency. Evaluated in a zero-shot setting on the challenging UG-MathBench dataset, with GPT-4o as its backbone, RCMR-Math significantly outperforms state-of-the-art LLMs. It achieves a substantial improvement in EAcc and a marked reduction in the Reasoning Gap. These results demonstrate that RCMR-Math effectively enhances the cross-version consistency and accuracy of LLM-based mathematical reasoning, setting a new benchmark for robust problem-solving in undergraduate mathematics.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781316809600.005","title":["Crossing the Boundaries: Collective Memory, Individual Memory, and Immigration"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781316809600.005","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2017,6,6]]},"container-title":["Collective Remembering"]},{"DOI":"10.7551\/mitpress\/9173.003.0006","title":["Defining Collective Memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7551\/mitpress\/9173.003.0006","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2012,2,24]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2012,2,24]]},"container-title":["Individual and Collective Memory Consolidation"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/bs.pbr.2022.07.005","title":["Habits of collective memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/bs.pbr.2022.07.005","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022]]},"container-title":["Progress in Brain Research","Collective Memory"]},{"DOI":"10.2307\/jj.30945960.7","title":["Working Memory Theories and Models"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2307\/jj.30945960.7","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2016,6,6]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2016,6,6]]},"container-title":["Working Memory and Second Language Learning"]},{"DOI":"10.2307\/j.ctv209xng5.6","title":["Collective Memory and Unremembering"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2307\/j.ctv209xng5.6","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021,9,27]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,9,27]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory and the Dutch East Indies"]},{"DOI":"10.1109\/icsmc.2011.6083904","title":["Modeling and analyzing agents' collective options in collaborative decision making situations using the constrained rationality framework"],"author":[{"given":"Majed","family":"Al-Shawa","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1109\/icsmc.2011.6083904","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2011,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2011,10]]},"container-title":["2011 IEEE International Conference on Systems, Man, and Cybernetics"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2026.104762","title":["Better language models better model the N400, but not reading time"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0003-2913-1103","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"James A.","family":"Michaelov","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-9395-9151","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Benjamin K.","family":"Bergen","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2026.104762","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,8]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.5772\/8379","title":["Skipping-Based Collaborative Recommendations inspired from Statistical Language Modeling"],"author":[{"given":"Geoffray","family":"Bonnin","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Armelle","family":"Brun","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Anne","family":"Boyer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5772\/8379","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2010,3,1]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2010,3,1]]},"container-title":["Web Intelligence and Intelligent Agents"]},{"DOI":"10.1006\/jmla.2000.2715","title":["Theory and Operational Definitions in Computational Memory Models: A Response to Glenberg and Robertson"],"author":[{"given":"Curt","family":"Burgess","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1006\/jmla.2000.2715","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2000,10]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2000,10]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.18653\/v1\/2025.realm-1.30","title":["From Knowledge to Noise: CTIM-Rover and the Pitfalls of Episodic Memory in Software Engineering Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Tobias","family":"Lindenbauer","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Georg","family":"Groh","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Hinrich","family":"Schuetze","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.18653\/v1\/2025.realm-1.30","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 1st Workshop for Research on Agent Language Models (REALM 2025)"]},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9780203774427-16","title":["Nostalgia, Immigration, and Collective Memory"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9780203774427-16","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2013,6,17]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2013,6,17]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory of Political Events"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0012596800003636","title":["Exploring Text-Generating Large Language Models (LLMs) for Emotion Recognition in Affective Intelligent Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Aaron","family":"Pico","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Valencian Research Institute for Artificial Intelligence (VRAIN), Universitat Polit \u00e8cnica de Val\u00e8ncia, Valencia, Spain, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Emilio","family":"Vivancos","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Valencian Research Institute for Artificial Intelligence (VRAIN), Universitat Polit \u00e8cnica de Val\u00e8ncia, Valencia, Spain, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Ana","family":"Garcia-Fornes","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Valencian Research Institute for Artificial Intelligence (VRAIN), Universitat Polit \u00e8cnica de Val\u00e8ncia, Valencia, Spain, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Vicente","family":"Botti","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Valencian Research Institute for Artificial Intelligence (VRAIN), Universitat Polit \u00e8cnica de Val\u00e8ncia, Valencia, Spain, --- Select a Country ---"},{"name":"Valencian Graduate School and Research Network of Artificial Intelligence (valgrAI), Spain, --- Select a Country ---"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0012596800003636","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 16th International Conference on Agents and Artificial Intelligence"]},{"DOI":"10.1353\/mlr.2024.a923583","title":["Towards a Collaborative Memory: German Memory Work in a Transnational Context by Sara Jones (review)"],"author":[{"given":"Bill","family":"Niven","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1353\/mlr.2024.a923583","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,4]]},"container-title":["Modern Language Review"]},{"DOI":"10.1515\/9789048553075-004","title":["1 Collective Memory and Unremembering"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1515\/9789048553075-004","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2021,12,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2021,12,31]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory and the Dutch East Indies"]},{"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.7170898","title":["Hierarchical Adaptive Memory for Language Models: A Tiered, Utility-Driven Memory Layer with Variable-Precision Consolidation"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0000-6576-0252","authenticated-orcid":true,"given":"KAM CHIU","family":"SZETO","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.7170898","issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Background: Traditional large language models (LLM) have frozen post-training weights, so they cannot naturally adapt to evolving knowledge and require computationally expensive fine-tuning to update. External retrieval mitigates this but shifts cost onto the query path and treats the store as passive text rather than in a structured, compressible state. Objectives: We propose Hierarchical Adaptive Memory (HAM), an in-built memory layer or external augmentation for foundation models that dynamically compresses and tiers information into working, episodic, and semantic memory based on entropy, allocating fewer bits to store and retrieve frequent, redundant content, while more to rare events. This predictive coding compression shall greatly reduce the memory block's byte footprint with a similar fine-tuning or memory teaching budget to reach a knowledge-accuracy target. While prior hierarchical memory approaches proposed operation at the tokenization level of an external memory system, HAM manages memory at the prototype level, enabling this mechanism to serve as an external store, a KV-cache compressor, or an internal memory block. Methods: HAM was tested under three integration modes: as an external memory system alongside a frozen model, as a replacement of the model's own key-value (KV) cache, or as an internal memory block fused into the residual stream. We studied it through four proof-of-concept experiments: an inference-time and memory compression comparison as external-memory on SmolLM2-135M-Instruct, a KV cache compression trial on the same frozen model with a per-condition compression-strength sweep, a toy-architecture with in-built memory-block experiment under a controlled redundancy lever, and a post-hoc cost-to-target and weight-drift analysis on the toy model with HAM versus standard flat memory block. In every experiment, the memory policy is the sole independent variable under a case-control trial. Results: In the external-memory trial on the LongMemEval benchmark, HAM matched uncompressed retrieval identically on task score and retrieval recall@k (\ud835\udc5d=1.000), while reducing physical store bytes by \u223c26.9%. The KV-cache compression study showed that at aggressive compression, HAM's frequency-weighted position retention preserved more next-token agreement than a random-retention ablation (0.411 vs 0.239), and the gap widened with context redundancy. The toy-architecture memory-block trial found that the HAM block's byte advantage over a flat block grew with redundancy, with byte-ratio 0.012\u00d7 to 0.005\u00d7 as redundancy increased, at matched recall quality. In the post-hoc cost-to-target analysis, the HAM memory block matched the standard flat memory block's optimizer steps, supervised tokens, and weight drift at a parity quality target, while storing fewer bytes. Conclusions: HAM demonstrates that predictive coding compression via a tiered model's memory, whether an external store, the KV cache, or an internal memory block, can reduce byte footprint at matched quality and possibly lower training cost on batch training, with the advantage growing with information redundancy. Scaling to larger models or combining with other techniques remains future work.<\/jats:p>"},{"DOI":"10.4324\/9781003497332-3","title":["Collective memory as an ideological construct"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4573-1136","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Karina V.","family":"Korostelina","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.4324\/9781003497332-3","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,8,30]]},"container-title":["Memory Sites and Conflict Dynamics"]},{"DOI":"10.1017\/9781108955638.005","title":["Models and Measures"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1017\/9781108955638.005","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2022,7,31]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2022,7,31]]},"container-title":["The Cambridge Handbook of Working Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.5220\/0015083300004018","title":["Tuning Large Language Models for Building Specialized Agents for Legal Texts"],"author":[{"given":"In\u00eas","family":"Esteves","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Polytechnic University of Viana do Castelo, Rua Escola Industrial e Comercial Nun\u2019 \u00c1lvares, 34, 4900-347, Viana do Castelo, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"A.","family":"Rosado da Cruz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Polytechnic University of Viana do Castelo, Rua Escola Industrial e Comercial Nun\u2019 \u00c1lvares, 34, 4900-347, Viana do Castelo, Portugal"},{"name":"ADiT-Lab, Polytechnic University of Viana do Castelo, Av. do Atl\u00e2ntico 644, 4900-348, Viana do Castelo, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Estrela","family":"Cruz","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Polytechnic University of Viana do Castelo, Rua Escola Industrial e Comercial Nun\u2019 \u00c1lvares, 34, 4900-347, Viana do Castelo, Portugal"},{"name":"ADiT-Lab, Polytechnic University of Viana do Castelo, Av. do Atl\u00e2ntico 644, 4900-348, Viana do Castelo, Portugal"},{"name":"Algoritmi Research Centre, Universidade do Minho, Guimar\u00e3es, Portugal"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5220\/0015083300004018","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026]]},"container-title":["Proceedings of the 28th International Conference on Enterprise Information Systems"]},{"DOI":"10.1016\/j.jml.2007.08.003","title":["Working memory in developing and applying mental models from spatial descriptions\u2606"],"author":[{"given":"Tad T.","family":"Bruny\u00e9","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Holly A.","family":"Taylor","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1016\/j.jml.2007.08.003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2008,4]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2008,4]]},"container-title":["Journal of Memory and Language"]},{"DOI":"10.7208\/chicago\/9780226399294.003.0003","title":["Analyzing Collective Memory"],"author":[{"given":"Jeffrey Andrew","family":"Barash","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.7208\/chicago\/9780226399294.003.0003","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2016]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2016]]},"container-title":["Collective Memory and the Historical Past"]},{"DOI":"10.21267\/aquilo.2020.69.46465","title":["\u0420\u041e\u041a-\u041a\u0423\u041b\u042c\u0422\u0423\u0420\u0410 \u0412 \u041a\u041e\u041b\u041b\u0415\u041a\u0422\u0418\u0412\u041d\u041e\u0419 \u041f\u0410\u041c\u042f\u0422\u0418 \u0415\u0415 \u041f\u0420\u0415\u0414\u0421\u0422\u0410\u0412\u0418\u0422\u0415\u041b\u0415\u0419"],"author":[{"given":"\u0421.\u0413.","family":"\u0414\u044e\u043a\u0438\u043d","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"\u041f\u0435\u0440\u043c\u0441\u043a\u0438\u0439 \u0433\u043e\u0441\u0443\u0434\u0430\u0440\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0435\u043d\u043d\u044b\u0439 \u0438\u043d\u0441\u0442\u0438\u0442\u0443\u0442 \u043a\u0443\u043b\u044c\u0442\u0443\u0440\u044b"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"\u0418.\u0412.","family":"\u0421\u0430\u043c\u043e\u0439\u043b\u043e\u0432\u0430","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"\u041f\u0435\u0440\u043c\u0441\u043a\u0438\u0439 \u0433\u043e\u0441\u0443\u0434\u0430\u0440\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0435\u043d\u043d\u044b\u0439 \u043d\u0430\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u044c\u043d\u044b\u0439 \u0438\u0441\u0441\u043b\u0435\u0434\u043e\u0432\u0430\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u0441\u043a\u0438\u0439 \u0443\u043d\u0438\u0432\u0435\u0440\u0441\u0438\u0442\u0435\u0442"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.21267\/aquilo.2020.69.46465","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,1,7]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,1,7]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>\u0421\u0442\u0430\u0442\u044c\u044f \u0441\u0432\u044f\u0437\u0430\u043d\u0430 \u0441 \u043d\u0430\u0443\u0447\u043d\u043e\u0439 \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0431\u043b\u0435\u043c\u043e\u0439, \u043a\u043e\u0442\u043e\u0440\u0430\u044f \u0437\u0430\u043a\u043b\u044e\u0447\u0430\u0435\u0442\u0441\u044f \u0432 \u043f\u0440\u0435\u043e\u0431\u0440\u0430\u0437\u043e\u0432\u0430\u043d\u0438\u0438 \u043a\u0443\u043b\u044c\u0442\u0443\u0440\u043d\u043e\u0433\u043e \u044f\u0432\u043b\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f \u0432 \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0446\u0435\u0441\u0441\u0435 \u0435\u0433\u043e \u0432\u0441\u043f\u043e\u043c\u0438\u043d\u0430\u043d\u0438\u044f. \u0412 \u0434\u0430\u043d\u043d\u043e\u043c \u0441\u043b\u0443\u0447\u0430\u0435 \u0440\u0435\u0447\u044c \u0438\u0434\u0435\u0442 \u043e \u0440\u043e\u043a-\u043a\u0443\u043b\u044c\u0442\u0443\u0440\u0435 \u0438 \u0440\u043e\u043a-\u043c\u0443\u0437\u044b\u043a\u0435, \u0441\u044b\u0433\u0440\u0430\u0432\u0448\u0438\u0445 \u0437\u043d\u0430\u0447\u0438\u0442\u0435\u043b\u044c\u043d\u0443\u044e \u0440\u043e\u043b\u044c \u0432 \u043f\u0440\u0435\u043e\u0431\u0440\u0430\u0437\u043e\u0432\u0430\u043d\u0438\u044f\u0445 1980-\u0445  \u043d\u0430\u0447\u0430\u043b\u0430 1990-\u0445 \u0433\u0433. \u0432 \u0421\u0421\u0421\u0420 \u0438 \u043f\u043e\u0441\u0442\u0441\u043e\u0432\u0435\u0442\u0441\u043a\u043e\u0439 \u0420\u043e\u0441\u0441\u0438\u0438. \u0412 \u043e\u0441\u043d\u043e\u0432\u0435 \u0433\u0438\u043f\u043e\u0442\u0435\u0437\u044b \u043b\u0435\u0436\u0438\u0442 \u0438\u0434\u0435\u044f \u0442\u043e\u0433\u043e, \u0447\u0442\u043e \u0438\u0437\u043c\u0435\u043d\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0444\u0443\u043d\u043a\u0446\u0438\u043e\u043d\u0430\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e\u0439 \u0440\u043e\u043b\u0438 \u0440\u043e\u043a-\u043c\u0443\u0437\u044b\u043a\u0438 \u0432 \u043f\u043e\u0441\u043b\u0435\u0434\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0434\u0432\u0430 \u0434\u0435\u0441\u044f\u0442\u0438\u043b\u0435\u0442\u0438\u044f \u043d\u0430\u0448\u043b\u043e \u043e\u0442\u0440\u0430\u0436\u0435\u043d\u0438\u0435 \u0432 \u0440\u0435\u0442\u0440\u043e\u0441\u043f\u0435\u043a\u0442\u0438\u0432\u043d\u043e\u043c \u0432\u043e\u0441\u043f\u0440\u0438\u044f\u0442\u0438\u0438 \u044d\u0442\u043e\u0433\u043e \u044f\u0432\u043b\u0435\u043d\u0438\u044f \u043b\u044e\u0434\u044c\u043c\u0438, \u0441\u0432\u044f\u0437\u0430\u043d\u043d\u044b\u043c\u0438 \u0441 \u0440\u043e\u043a-\u043a\u0443\u043b\u044c\u0442\u0443\u0440\u043e\u0439. \u041e\u0441\u043d\u043e\u0432\u043d\u044b\u043c\u0438 \u043f\u043e\u0437\u0438\u0446\u0438\u044f\u043c\u0438, \u0432\u044b\u0440\u0430\u0436\u0435\u043d\u043d\u044b\u0445 \u0432 \u0432\u043e\u0441\u043f\u043e\u043c\u0438\u043d\u0430\u043d\u0438\u044f\u0445, \u043f\u0440\u0435\u0434\u0441\u0442\u0430\u044e\u0442 \u043a\u043e\u043d\u0444\u043b\u0438\u043a\u0442 \u043c\u0435\u0436\u0434\u0443 \u0440\u043e\u043a\u043e\u043c \u0438 \u0441\u043e\u0432\u0435\u0442\u0441\u043a\u043e\u0439 \u043a\u0443\u043b\u044c\u0442\u0443\u0440\u043e\u0439, \u0432\u0437\u0430\u0438\u043c\u043e\u0441\u0432\u044f\u0437\u044c \u0440\u043e\u043a-\u043c\u0443\u0437\u044b\u043a\u0438 \u0438 \u043f\u043e\u043b\u0438\u0442\u0438\u0447\u0435\u0441\u043a\u0438\u0445 \u043a\u043e\u043b\u043b\u0438\u0437\u0438\u0439 \u044d\u043f\u043e\u0445\u0438 \u043f\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0441\u0442\u0440\u043e\u0439\u043a\u0438, \u043c\u043e\u043b\u043e\u0434\u0435\u0436\u043d\u044b\u0435 \u0441\u0443\u0431\u043a\u0443\u043b\u044c\u0442\u0443\u0440\u044b \u043a\u043e\u043d\u0446\u0430 80-\u0445 \u0433\u0433. \u0438 \u0441\u0442\u0435\u043f\u0435\u043d\u044c \u0438\u0437\u043e\u043b\u044f\u0446\u0438\u0438 \u0440\u043e\u043a-\u0441\u0440\u0435\u0434\u044b \u043e\u0442 \u0441\u043e\u0446\u0438\u0430\u043b\u044c\u043d\u043e\u0433\u043e \u0431\u043e\u043b\u044c\u0448\u0438\u043d\u0441\u0442\u0432\u0430.<\/jats:p>\n                                                                                            <jats:p>The article is connected with scientific problem that lies in the transformation of cultural phenomena in the process of remembering it. In this case we talk about rock-culture and rock-music. In had importance in transformation of the Soviet society in 80s and in the first years of Post-Soviet society. Our hypothesis is idea that changing of functions of rock-music in last time is reflected in retrospective perception of this phenomenon by men who are connected with rock-culture. The main positions of remembering are conflict between the Rock and the Soviet culture, connection of rock-music and political facts of Perestroyka, the youthful sub-cultures of the end of the 1980s, the degree of isolation of the rock invironment from the social majority.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["\u0414\u0438\u0430\u043b\u043e\u0433 \u0441\u043e \u0432\u0440\u0435\u043c\u0435\u043d\u0435\u043c"]},{"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2020,9,15]]},"abstract":"<p>The first chapter examines the phenomenal rise to popularity of Walter Scott\u2019s historical fiction throughout the transatlantic world, in the context of its evocation of collective memory, which was particularly suited to the anxieties of emergent national cultures. In <italic>Waverley<\/italic>, Scott employs mnemonic tropes to install a particular mode of modern historical consciousness: the \u2018aftermath\u2019, in which the present time is shaped in terms of its proximate relation (measured out in one or two human lifespans) to a moment of historical crux. The time of the Forty-Five is imagined as the catalyst for abrupt and transformational social and cultural change, which not only determined the nation\u2019s present but altered the very pace of history. This chapter shows how <italic>Waverley<\/italic>\u2019s example of the \u2018aftermath\u2019 provided tropes, models and theoretical frameworks for North American writers for their own expression of national memory. Examples include Washington Irving\u2019s \u2018Rip Van Winkle\u2019, John Neal\u2019s <italic>Seventy-Six,<\/italic> and Philippe-Joseph Aubert de Gasp\u00e9\u2019s <italic>Les Anciens Canadiens<\/italic>. The chapter also traces the literary development of the \u2018aftermath\u2019 in the Scottish \u2018memorial\u2019 writing of Robert Chambers and Henry Cockburn.<\/p>","DOI":"10.3366\/edinburgh\/9781474455466.003.0002","title":["Aftermaths: Walter Scott and Imagining Collective Memory in the Transatlantic World"],"author":[{"given":"Kenneth","family":"McNeil","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2021,5,20]]},"container-title":["Scottish Romanticism and Collective Memory in the British Atlantic"],"issued":{"date-parts":[[2020,9,15]]},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.3366\/edinburgh\/9781474455466.003.0002"},{"DOI":"10.1037\/xge0001698.supp","title":["Supplemental Material for Collaborative Recall Changes the Global Organization of Memory: A Representational Similarity Analysis of Social Influences on Individual and Collective Memory Organization"],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1037\/xge0001698.supp","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,3,20]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Journal of Experimental Psychology: General"]},{"DOI":"10.22158\/eltls.v6n2p243","title":["An Analysis of the the Mutual Construction of Individual Memory and Collective Memory in Atonement"],"author":[{"given":"Xiaohan","family":"Zhao","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22158\/eltls.v6n2p243","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,25]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024,4,25]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>Atonement is one of the masterpieces of British author Ian McEwan, which has attracted a lot of attention since its publication, and this novel won him the Pulitzer Prize for Literature. The novel\u2019s protagonist, Briony, makes a big mistake as an adolescent by indulging her imagination, breaking up her sister Cecilia and Robbie, and sending Robbie to prison. Robbie goes from being an Oxford student to a prisoner, and then he goes to the front line in the midst of World War II to get out of prison early. Cecilia, on the other hand, after known this terrible news, breaks with her family to become a nurse. Cecilia and Robbie are finally reunited at last. Briony finds them and repents deeply, telling the truth and apologizes to her sister and Robbie. It seems like an old fashioned happy-ending novel, except none of this reunion is reality. Just as the readers are immersed in the sense of redemption and happiness, the author ends the book with a meta-fictional message telling the readers that the previous happy ending is just a made-up one by Briony in her head, and she writes it down as if it is truly happened , in order to atone for her own sins. She changes the fate of two people and spends fifty-nine years in penance. Previous research angles of scholars at home and abroad mainly focus on narrative art, metafictional ending, war and trauma, the theme of atonement, etc., and what exactly makes Briony made a big mistake, and how she carries out self-salvation, this will take Briony, the main character of the novel, as the object of study, and use Halbwachs\u2019 theory of individual and collective memory to explore her existential dilemma and her self-salvation through the freedom of choice to her self-rescue from her predicament, aiming to reveal McEwan\u2019s use of memory theory and his thoughts on human nature.<\/jats:p>","container-title":["English Language Teaching and Linguistics Studies"]},{"DOI":"10.48106\/dial.v78.i3.02","title":["Collective Regret and Collective Obligations without Collective Agents"],"author":[{"given":"Bill","family":"Wringe","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.48106\/dial.v78.i3.02","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"container-title":["Dialectica"]},{"DOI":"10.1007\/978-3-031-92285-5_8","title":["Large Language Model Agents"],"author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0002-4626-826X","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jerin George","family":"Mathew","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0005-0100-0605","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Jacopo","family":"Rossi","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1007\/978-3-031-92285-5_8","published-print":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"published-online":{"date-parts":[[2025,7,30]]},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025]]},"container-title":["Engineering Information Systems with Large Language Models"]}],"items-per-page":100,"query":{"start-index":0,"search-terms":null}}}